'''''Nephrolepis''''' is a genus of about 30 species of [[fern]]s. It is the only genus in the family '''Nephrolepidaceae''', placed in the suborder [[Aspleniineae]] (eupolypods I) of the order [[Polypodiales]] in the [[Pteridophyte Phylogeny Group]] classification of 2016 (PPG I).<ref name=PPGI/> (It is placed in the [[Dryopteridaceae]] in some other classifications.<ref>[http://plants.usda.gov/plant-profile?symbol=NEPHR USDA:  ''Nephrolepis'']; family [[Dryopteridaceae]]. accessed 7 Sept. 2016.</ref>) Species in this genus include plants commonly referred to as '''Boston ferns'''.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Schott |first1=L. |title=Nephrolepis exaltata |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:17160830-1/general-information#vernacular-names |website=Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew: Plants of the World Online |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew |access-date=11 July 2025}}</ref> The fronds are long and narrow, and [[once-pinnate]], in the case of one Bornean species reaching thirty feet (nine meters) in length.<ref>{{cite journal |  last1= Finn | first1= Judith | last2= Parsons | first2= Jerry | date= Spring 1996 | title= A Mind-Boggling Variety of Life | journal=Newsletter of the Univ. Of Calif. Botanic Gardens at Berkeley |volume= 21 | issue= 2 | page= 2}}</ref>

